---
import Arrow from "$lib/components/Arrow.svelte";
import Toc from "$lib/components/Toc.astro";
import Morafeno from "$lib/about/Morafeno.astro";
import NameJoin from "$lib/about/NameJoin.svelte";
import PcodeLevels from "$lib/about/PcodeLevels.astro";
import PopulationMaps from "$lib/about/PopulationMaps.svelte";
import Timeline from "$lib/about/Timeline.astro";
import UseStrip from "$lib/about/UseStrip.astro";
import { base, standardsBase, toolsBase } from "$lib/utils/paths";
import PageLayout from "../layouts/PageLayout.astro";

const summary =
  "Common Operational Datasets (CODs) are the administrative boundaries (COD-AB) and population statistics (COD-PS) shared by the organizations taking part in a humanitarian response.";

const sections = [
  { slug: "why", text: "Why CODs exist" },
  { slug: "preparedness", text: "Preparedness" },
  { slug: "p-codes", text: "P-codes" },
  { slug: "use", text: "How CODs are used" },
];
const heading = Object.fromEntries(sections.map((s) => [s.slug, s.text]));
---

<PageLayout title="About CODs" summary={summary}>
  <div class="wrap prose-body">
    <article class="prose">
      <h2 id="why">{heading.why}</h2>
      <p>
        In emergencies, many organizations provide relief, each in its own way, and each collects
        information as the response goes on: needs assessments, where partners are working, how many
        people have been reached. Sharing this information is how needs get identified and how gaps
        and duplication in assistance are avoided. It only works if every organization refers to
        places in the same way.
      </p>
      <p>
        CODs give partners that common reference. When UN agencies, NGOs, the Red Cross and Red
        Crescent, and governments use the same boundaries and population figures, their data can be
        compared and combined. In each country, the Information Management Working Group, made up of
        information managers from government, UN agencies, clusters and NGOs, adopts the CODs as the
        standard.
      </p>

      <h2 id="preparedness">{heading.preparedness}</h2>
      <p>
        Decisions in the first hours and days of an emergency rely on data that already exists, so
        the work of gathering, managing and sharing it has to happen before a crisis. For
        sudden-onset emergencies, current guidance calls for CODs to be shared within 48 hours. That
        leaves time to check and correct them, but not to build them. If CODs have been kept up to
        date, the corrections are small.
      </p>
      <Timeline />

      <h2 id="p-codes">{heading["p-codes"]}</h2>
      <p>
        Linking datasets by place name causes errors. Sources spell names differently, or use another
        language: the Madagascar COD-AB has Amoron I Mania and Haute Matsiatra, where OpenStreetMap
        has Amoron’i Mania and Matsiatra Ambony. Names also repeat. In Madagascar, 81 different
        administrative level 4 units are called Morafeno.
      </p>
      <Morafeno />
      <p>
        A P-code (place code) identifies one administrative unit. It starts with the country’s ISO3
        code and adds a three-digit number for each level below, separated by dots. Units that
        share a name, like the commune of Morafeno and the fokontany inside it, still get different
        codes.
      </p>
      <PcodeLevels />
      <p>
        Datasets that use the same P-codes can be joined directly, without matching names by hand.
        Each COD-AB includes a gazetteer listing every P-code with its name. The <a
          href={`${standardsBase}specification/pcode/`}>P-code format</a
        > in the Standards gives the full rules.
      </p>
      <NameJoin client:visible />

      <h2 id="use">{heading.use}</h2>
      <p>COD-AB and COD-PS are used in every phase of the humanitarian programme cycle:</p>
      <UseStrip />
      <p>
        The maps below link the two. Each COD-PS figure is attached to its COD-AB boundary by
        P-code, which is enough to show where people live and how age and sex vary.
      </p>
      <PopulationMaps client:visible />

      <nav class="links" aria-label="Related">
        <a href={standardsBase}>Standards <Arrow /></a>
        <a href={toolsBase}>Tools <Arrow /></a>
        <a href={`${base}explorer/`}>Explorer <Arrow /></a>
      </nav>
    </article>

    <Toc headings={sections} />
  </div>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  .links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem 1.75rem;
    margin-top: 4rem;
    padding-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--cd-blue--lightest);
  }
  .links a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.25rem;
    color: var(--brand-primary--text);
    font-weight: 700;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .links a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
</style>
